#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199425} :activity/id #uuid "572640c2-48d2-5508-9b8f-be1309fc61e3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049076 :topic/id #uuid "d8ad0390-dfee-58d8-ac50-00cbfdc660af" :topic/math-academy-id 4393} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199577 :tutorial/content "As previously seen, we can define sets using so-called *constructive definitions* of the form\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ {\\color{blue}\\textrm{rule}} \\: : \\: {\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}} \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere the ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{rule}}$ is an expression (or formula) that depends on a variable that *constructs* elements of our set when the variable runs over some ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}}.$ \n\nFor example, the set $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ 2n+1\\, : \\, n\\in\\mathbb N_0 \\big \\}\n$$\n\nis defined constructively and contains all positive odd numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n+1\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}} \\\\  & ={2(0)+1,\\,2(1)+1,\\,2(2)+1,\\,2(3)+1,\\,…} \\\\  & ={1,3,5,7,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSometimes, it's helpful to write a set using a constructive definition. For example, how might we express the following set constructively?\n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ 4, 7, 10, 13, \\ldots \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n4,\\quad 7,\\quad 10,\\quad 13,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3 \\\\  & =7-4 \\\\  & =10-7 \\\\  & =13-10 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb N$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)\\cdot d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set constructively as follows:\n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ 3n+1 \\: : \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe expression $3n+1$ generates the elements of our set when $n$ runs over $\\mathbb{N}.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- A bar \"$\\mid$\" is just as acceptable as a colon to separate the rule from the base set. So, we could also express our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB=\\big\\{ 3n+1 \\: \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big\\}\n$$\n\n- The constructive definition of a given set is typically not unique. For example, we could also define $B$ as follows:\n\n$$\nB=\\big\\{ 3n+4 \\: :\\: n \\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used $\\mathbb N_0$ as the base set.\n\nThis definition does indeed generate all elements of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3n+4\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}} & ={3(0)+4,\\,3(1)+4,\\,3(2)+4,\\,3(3)+4,\\,…} \\\\  & ={4,\\,7,\\,10,\\,13,\\,…} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "57358da7-7aa7-5201-b3bd-e43581b18a41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8307 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "95633ec2-0a87-53d4-9845-589ad5e2c472" :step/math-academy-id 67909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198166 :question/id #uuid "231da77b-8370-543a-abf0-effb78e50802" :question/math-academy-id "e-13186"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5b3eb6-187f-5f16-a32e-66b0842adfe9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396295}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Given Set Using an Arithmetic Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "d31b48cd-0396-5a79-94df-84872a357ae8" :step/math-academy-id 67910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198464 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198164 :question/id #uuid "e81a9a3f-3dbb-5244-8ea0-9b967bb5a46f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13184"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9df722a8-2db6-5bb8-a69c-b81b33037ab2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396308}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Given Set Using a Geometric Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "01644308-2657-5bfc-bdad-7c6353f7293a" :step/math-academy-id 67913 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199552 :tutorial/content "We can also define sets using so-called *conditional definitions* of the form\n\n$$\n\\{ {\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}} \\: : \\: {\\color{blue}\\textrm{conditions}} \\}\n$$\n\nwhere our set contains only those elements of the ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}}$ that satisfy the given ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{conditions}}$ (or restrictions).\n\nFor example, the set $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = \\{n\\in \\mathbb Z \\::\\: -2\\leq n\\leq 2\\}\n$$\n\nis defined conditionally and contains all integers between $-2$ and $2$ inclusive:\n\n$$\nA = \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\n$$\n\nIt's often helpful to describe sets using conditional definitions. For example, let's rewrite the following set using a conditional definition.\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{50,51,52,53,\\ldots\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe set $S$ contains all natural numbers greater than or equal to $50,$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb N \\::\\: n\\geq 50\\}\n$$\n\nThe conditional definition of a set is not unique. We can write down an equivalent definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb N \\::\\: n\\gt 49\\}\n$$\n\nUnder this definition, $S$ contains all natural numbers greater than $49,$ *excluding* $49.$ \n\nAlthough $\\mathbb N$ is the most natural base set in this instance, we can also define $S$ using the set of integers $\\mathbb Z$ as the base set. For example,\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb Z \\::\\: n\\geq 50\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a5b5e889-bdb2-5707-90ab-be875064cc73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8228 :tutorial/title "Conditional Definitions of Sets"} :step/id #uuid "82de79d7-d2f1-5a78-845b-a3a4a47ee4c5" :step/math-academy-id 67916 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198465 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198150 :question/id #uuid "cfd5a7b6-4ee8-5c66-9b1d-81d1d9a1aee7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f49ef1c2-06de-597c-bf4d-6d613989987e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396272}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Set Using a Conditional Definition"} :step/id #uuid "5ed9a110-74bf-5cdf-8e45-8f23b994a145" :step/math-academy-id 67917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198466 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198165 :question/id #uuid "a67f56bb-352b-5126-84c8-c38fc3d0532c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13185"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad21350b-2e3b-57ee-a28c-32a7bd1b2513" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050455}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287857}] :knowledge-point/title "Defining a Set Conditionally Using the Roots of a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "03fd2408-ced4-5196-8d53-1d732ef57031" :step/math-academy-id 67920}] :activity/title "Describing Sets Using Set-Builder Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198534}]}
